Plausible ideas to enhance the player.
- Use the SN noise channel for simple drums (no pitch control).
- Use the SN noise channel in periodic mode, and write the noise register
within a low frequency block to control the periodic noise frequency. This
will be possible because writing to the noise register resets the noise
channel state, so it can be kept in a "playing but silent" state until the
blip is needed at whatever output frequency is desired.
- Play samples backwards. Same CPU power required, just different code and
a different sound. Probably possible to rewrite the forwards / backwards
code via self-modification at runtime.
- Dedicate one playback channel as a drum channel. This will playback at
constant rate and therefore take less CPU cycles to iterate.
- Dedicate one or more playback channels as synth channels. Square wave,
saw wave, etc. The samples are 256 bytes in size and can be iterated across
with fewer CPU cycles.
- Can mix in a couple of square waves very cheaply (2 cycles?) by updating a
constant in a low frequency block.
- Single-pole IIR low pass filter does not require many cycles...
- Modify the in-memory sample in a low frequency block. Could be simple, i.e.
change the pulse width of a square wave. Or could be something more
interesting!
- Use the VIA timer machinery, or even the shift register(!) as a source of
auto-updating simple waveforms. The shift register could be used to
approximate a sawtooth wave (0x80 -> 0x40 -> 0x20 -> ...) with no CPU
cycles required to advance the waveform.
- Double the storage for pre-computed samples by packing each memory byte
with 4 bits each from two different samples and using the lookup table
machinery.
